Dear CRAN maintainers,

I submitted concurve 3.0.3 on 1 September 2026. Please could that
submission be discarded rather than published.

Since submitting, I found a correctness bug in curve_lik_glm(), a
function that is new in this version. For families with a free dispersion
parameter - gaussian, Gamma, inverse.gaussian, and the quasi families -
it profiled the unscaled deviance instead of dividing by the estimated
dispersion, as stats::confint.glm() does. The resulting likelihood
support intervals are wrong by a factor of 1/sqrt(dispersion), which
makes them depend on the units of the response variable: refitting the
same relationship with the response rescaled changes the interval.

Measured against confint(), a gaussian fit with residual SD 0.4 gave an
interval 219% of the correct width, and the same relationship with
residual SD 5.0 gave 19% - five times too narrow. Since gaussian is the
default family for glm(), an ordinary glm(y ~ x) is affected, and the
too-narrow direction overstates precision. For a package whose purpose is
communicating statistical uncertainty, I would rather not publish that
function in this state.

The fix, along with regression tests covering each affected family and
the rescaling equivariance the bug violated, is ready as 3.0.4. I will
submit it once 3.0.3 has been withdrawn. binomial and poisson results
were never affected, their dispersion being fixed at 1.

I apologise for the extra work this causes. concurve was archived in 2022
for uncorrected check problems, and I would rather catch this myself than
publish a function I know gives incorrect answers.

Thank you for your time and for maintaining CRAN.

Best regards,
